1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:07,660
Swift-footed Achilles, the greatest of the Greek heroes, is racing a tortoise, and the tortoise has a head start.

2
00:00:07,740 --> 00:00:13,200
Each time Achilles reaches the spot where the tortoise was, it has crept a little further on.

3
00:00:13,280 --> 00:00:19,120
So, says an argument nearly 2,500 years old, he can never catch it.

4
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:21,310
Of course, he does catch it.

5
00:00:21,390 --> 00:00:29,780
But saying exactly what's wrong with the argument took more than 2,000 years, and some philosophers say it still isn't settled.

6
00:00:29,860 --> 00:00:34,580
This is Zeno's paradox of Achilles and the tortoise.

7
00:00:38,780 --> 00:00:45,670
Zeno was born around 490 BC in Elea, a Greek city on the west coast of southern Italy.

8
00:00:45,750 --> 00:00:50,010
He was a student and friend of an older philosopher, Parmenides.

9
00:00:50,090 --> 00:00:56,360
Parmenides taught that reality is one unchanging whole, and that change and motion are illusions.

10
00:00:56,440 --> 00:01:02,830
Critics found that absurd. Zeno replied that their views, followed through, led to worse absurdities.

11
00:01:02,910 --> 00:01:10,720
This kind of argument is called reductio ad absurdum, and Aristotle called Zeno the inventor of dialectic.

12
00:01:10,800 --> 00:01:15,370
Zeno's book is lost. Fewer than 200 of his own words survive.

13
00:01:15,450 --> 00:01:22,640
We know his arguments from others, such as Plato, Aristotle, and Simplicius, writing a thousand years later.

14
00:01:22,720 --> 00:01:30,590
Plato even has the two visit Athens to meet a young Socrates, though he probably invented that meeting.

15
00:01:33,190 --> 00:01:38,030
The most famous argument is a race, as Aristotle gives it in his Physics.

16
00:01:38,110 --> 00:01:46,130
"In a race, the quickest runner can never overtake the slowest, since the pursuer must first reach the point whence the pursued started,"

17
00:01:46,210 --> 00:01:48,970
"so that the slower must always hold a lead."

18
00:01:49,050 --> 00:01:55,610
There's no tortoise in that version, just the slowest runner. The tortoise comes from later retellings.

19
00:01:55,690 --> 00:02:01,430
So let's race. Achilles runs 10 metres a second, and the tortoise 1 metre a second.

20
00:02:01,510 --> 00:02:04,600
And the tortoise starts 100 metres ahead.

21
00:02:04,680 --> 00:02:10,140
In stage one, Achilles runs those 100 metres, which takes him 10 seconds.

22
00:02:10,220 --> 00:02:14,590
But in those 10 seconds, the tortoise has crawled 10 metres further.

23
00:02:14,670 --> 00:02:21,280
In stage two, Achilles covers those 10 metres in 1 second, and the tortoise crawls another metre.

24
00:02:21,360 --> 00:02:27,000
Stage three leaves a gap of 10 centimetres, and stage four leaves just 1 centimetre.

25
00:02:27,080 --> 00:02:33,090
Each gap is a tenth of the one before. It shrinks forever, but it never reaches zero.

26
00:02:33,170 --> 00:02:39,670
So there is always one more stage. Achilles, Zeno concludes, never catches up.

27
00:02:43,470 --> 00:02:48,510
So how far does Achilles run before he draws level? Let's add up the stages.

28
00:02:48,590 --> 00:02:54,280
It's 100 metres, plus 10, plus 1, plus a tenth, plus a hundredth, and so on.

29
00:02:54,360 --> 00:03:04,230
The running totals are 100, then 110, then 111, then 111.1, then 111.11.

30
00:03:04,310 --> 00:03:09,880
They close in on 111 and one ninth metres, with the ones repeating forever.

31
00:03:09,960 --> 00:03:14,500
The times add up the same way, to 11 and one ninth seconds.

32
00:03:14,580 --> 00:03:20,350
Infinitely many stages, but a finite distance and a finite time.

33
00:03:20,430 --> 00:03:29,920
Plot distance against time, and each runner is a straight line. The lines cross at 11.1 seconds and 111.1 metres.

34
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:36,040
Zeno's stages are a staircase closing in on that crossing, and every one of them comes before it.

35
00:03:36,120 --> 00:03:42,920
The moment Achilles catches up isn't one of the stages. It comes just after all of them.

36
00:03:43,120 --> 00:03:49,140
It's often said that Zeno thought infinitely many pieces must add up to something infinite.

37
00:03:49,220 --> 00:03:53,610
But no ancient source has Zeno discussing an infinite sum at all.

38
00:03:53,690 --> 00:04:01,230
In Simplicius's report, his worry is that it is impossible "to traverse an infinite number of things in a finite time."

39
00:04:01,310 --> 00:04:08,860
So the puzzle isn't the total. It's how anyone can finish infinitely many steps, with no last one.

40
00:04:11,460 --> 00:04:15,280
Zeno's dichotomy makes the same point without a tortoise.

41
00:04:15,360 --> 00:04:22,880
In Aristotle's words, "that which is in locomotion must arrive at the half-way stage before it arrives at the goal."

42
00:04:22,960 --> 00:04:29,070
So to run 100 metres, first run half of it. Then half of what's left, then half of that.

43
00:04:29,150 --> 00:04:35,970
The running totals are one half, three quarters, seven eighths, fifteen sixteenths, creeping towards the whole.

44
00:04:36,050 --> 00:04:44,290
After 10 halvings, you've run 1,023 parts in 1,024, and less than 10 centimetres is left.

45
00:04:44,370 --> 00:04:51,540
After 20, less than a tenth of a millimetre is left. But there is always a half still to run.

46
00:04:51,620 --> 00:04:58,010
There's a nastier version. Before the half-way mark, you must reach the quarter mark, and before that, the eighth.

47
00:04:58,090 --> 00:05:02,180
Then the run has no first step at all. So how does it ever begin?

48
00:05:02,260 --> 00:05:10,860
Aristotle noted that the Achilles is much the same argument. In our race, the gaps shrink by tenths, not halves.

49
00:05:13,460 --> 00:05:18,620
Zeno's arrow attacks motion from the other side, by cutting time into instants.

50
00:05:18,700 --> 00:05:22,170
Take an arrow in flight, and freeze a single instant.

51
00:05:22,250 --> 00:05:27,570
In that instant, it fills a space exactly its own size, so it isn't moving.

52
00:05:27,650 --> 00:05:33,040
But the whole flight is made of instants like this one. So when does the arrow move?

53
00:05:33,120 --> 00:05:39,810
Aristotle replied that time is not made of indivisible nows, any more than a line is made of points.

54
00:05:39,890 --> 00:05:43,760
The modern answer treats speed at an instant as a limit.

55
00:05:43,840 --> 00:05:49,360
Say it flies at 50 metres a second. In a tenth of a second, it goes 5 metres.

56
00:05:49,440 --> 00:05:56,930
In a hundredth, it goes half a metre. Distance over time stays at 50 metres a second, however short the interval.

57
00:05:57,010 --> 00:06:03,000
In a single instant it goes nowhere, and zero divided by zero isn't a number at all.

58
00:06:03,080 --> 00:06:06,820
So the speed at an instant is defined as that limit.

59
00:06:06,900 --> 00:06:14,070
On this view, motion doesn't happen inside an instant. It is being in different places at different times.

60
00:06:14,150 --> 00:06:20,740
The last of the four, the stadium, has rows of equal blocks passing each other in opposite directions.

61
00:06:20,820 --> 00:06:26,640
The front block of one row passes every oncoming block, but only half of the still ones.

62
00:06:26,720 --> 00:06:31,910
From this, Zeno concluded that half a given time is equal to double that time.

63
00:06:31,990 --> 00:06:39,880
The usual verdict is that he ignored relative speed: the oncoming blocks approach twice as fast.

64
00:06:43,480 --> 00:06:47,450
One famous reply to Zeno wasn't an argument at all.

65
00:06:47,530 --> 00:06:54,970
Simplicius tells a story: when Diogenes the Cynic heard Zeno's arguments, he said nothing, stood up, and walked.

66
00:06:55,050 --> 00:07:01,420
That kind of answer has a Latin name, solvitur ambulando: it is solved by walking.

67
00:07:01,500 --> 00:07:08,190
But walking shows only that the conclusion is false. It doesn't show which step of the argument fails.

68
00:07:08,270 --> 00:07:16,360
Aristotle noted that shorter distances take shorter times. But then the time, too, is made of infinitely many pieces.

69
00:07:16,440 --> 00:07:19,550
So his deeper answer splits infinity in two.

70
00:07:19,630 --> 00:07:25,790
An actual infinity would be a completed collection of infinitely many things, all there at once.

71
00:07:25,870 --> 00:07:31,310
A potential infinity is a process that can always go on, but is never completed.

72
00:07:31,390 --> 00:07:38,930
A path, he said, holds infinitely many halves only potentially. They'd be actual only if the runner stopped at each one.

73
00:07:39,010 --> 00:07:45,350
In his words, "if the units are actual, it is not possible: if they are potential, it is possible."

74
00:07:45,430 --> 00:07:52,200
That answer satisfied nearly all scholars for about 1,500 years.

75
00:07:54,800 --> 00:07:58,960
Meanwhile, mathematicians were learning to tame infinite sums.

76
00:07:59,040 --> 00:08:04,700
In the 3rd century BC, Archimedes found the area of a slice cut from a parabola.

77
00:08:04,780 --> 00:08:10,970
He filled it with triangles, and each new row of triangles has a quarter of the area of the row before.

78
00:08:11,050 --> 00:08:17,040
So the slice is the big triangle, times 1 plus a quarter plus a sixteenth, and so on.

79
00:08:17,120 --> 00:08:22,430
Archimedes showed that this comes to exactly four thirds of the big triangle.

80
00:08:22,510 --> 00:08:28,720
But infinite sums can be slippery. Take 1, minus 1, plus 1, minus 1, and so on.

81
00:08:28,800 --> 00:08:33,890
Pair the terms one way and you get 0. Pair them another way and you get 1.

82
00:08:33,970 --> 00:08:42,740
In 1821, the French mathematician Augustin-Louis Cauchy insisted on strict tests of when an infinite sum makes sense.

83
00:08:42,820 --> 00:08:48,140
Its value is the number its running totals settle down to, if they settle at all.

84
00:08:48,220 --> 00:08:55,060
Then a half plus a quarter plus an eighth is exactly 1, while 1 minus 1 plus 1 has no sum.

85
00:08:55,140 --> 00:09:02,630
In 1861, Karl Weierstrass gave the definition of a limit in the precise form used today.

86
00:09:02,710 --> 00:09:08,670
Pick any margin, however small. From some stage on, every running total stays within it.

87
00:09:08,750 --> 00:09:16,570
For a margin of a millimetre, Achilles' totals stay inside from stage 6 on. For a micrometre, from stage 9.

88
00:09:16,650 --> 00:09:27,620
So 111.1 metres is the limit of Zeno's stages. Some historians, such as Carl Boyer, say calculus solves the paradox.

89
00:09:30,220 --> 00:09:38,180
Some philosophers disagree. The maths says where and when Achilles draws level, but how does he finish infinitely many tasks?

90
00:09:38,260 --> 00:09:45,180
Bertrand Russell argued that Achilles really does complete infinitely many tasks, in a finite time.

91
00:09:45,260 --> 00:09:50,200
In 1954, the philosopher James Thomson replied with a lamp.

92
00:09:50,280 --> 00:09:56,350
Switch it on. After a minute, switch it off. After half a minute, on again, then off after a quarter.

93
00:09:56,430 --> 00:10:01,250
Each wait is half the last, and the waits add up to exactly 2 minutes.

94
00:10:01,330 --> 00:10:03,990
At 2 minutes, is the lamp on or off?

95
00:10:04,070 --> 00:10:11,080
It can't be on: every time it went on, it was switched off again. And off fails for the same reason.

96
00:10:11,160 --> 00:10:17,600
Thomson concluded that a supertask, infinitely many tasks in a finite time, is impossible.

97
00:10:17,680 --> 00:10:23,670
Its switch flips like the running totals of 1 minus 1 plus 1, which never settle.

98
00:10:23,750 --> 00:10:32,560
In 1962, Paul Benacerraf objected that the story fixes the lamp before 2 minutes, but says nothing about that moment itself.

99
00:10:32,640 --> 00:10:36,860
So there's no contradiction, just a question the story leaves open.

100
00:10:36,940 --> 00:10:42,980
Philosophers still disagree about what counts as a task, and whether a supertask is possible.

101
00:10:43,060 --> 00:10:50,050
And some theories of quantum gravity suggest that space and time may not divide endlessly at all.

102
00:10:50,130 --> 00:10:57,070
As the philosopher Kevin Brown put it, "it's probably foolhardy to think we've reached the end."

103
00:10:59,670 --> 00:11:01,430
So, back to the race.

104
00:11:01,510 --> 00:11:10,530
Achilles reaches the tortoise's start after 10 seconds, and draws level after 11.1 seconds, at 111.1 metres.

105
00:11:10,610 --> 00:11:18,770
Before that moment come infinitely many of Zeno's stages, and every one of them fits inside those 11.1 seconds.

106
00:11:18,850 --> 00:11:26,220
Zeno was wrong that motion is impossible. But he made us say what we mean by infinity, a sum, and an instant.

107
00:11:26,300 --> 00:11:34,760
Bertrand Russell wrote that Zeno's arguments have afforded grounds for almost all theories of space, time and infinity since.

108
00:11:34,840 --> 00:11:40,810
Achilles wins the race. Zeno's argument, it seems, is still running.
